WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.270
The school kitchen has a new rule:

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.510
every lunch must give pupils enough

3
00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.830
energy, protein and iron.

4
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.240
And the money is tight.

5
00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.190
The cook asked me: what’s the

6
00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.890
cheapest plate that’s still enough?

7
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.500
Today: from words to a model, so a hard

8
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.620
choice becomes numbers we can check.

9
00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:23.630
A model starts with the

10
00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:26.030
decisions, written as variables.

11
00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:30.190
Here: how many portions of each food.

12
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.780
Call them rice, beans, greens and

13
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.280
so on: each one a number, with a

14
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.800
unit, cups or handfuls.

15
00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.510
Next, each limit becomes an inequality.

16
00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.850
A cup of rice gives two hundred and sixty

17
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.090
kilocalories, a handful of groundnuts one

18
00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.050
hundred and seventy, and so on.

19
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.700
Add them up: the total must be

20
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.980
at least seven hundred.

21
00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.140
Protein must be at least twenty grams,

22
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.740
and iron at least five milligrams.

23
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.220
Then the goal, written as one

24
00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:07.560
expression: the cost.

25
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.580
Fifteen coins a cup of rice, eight a

26
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.780
handful of groundnuts, and so on.

27
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.240
Make it as small as possible.

28
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.720
Variables, constraints and an

29
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.140
objective: together, that’s a model.

30
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.000
Constraint is the maths word for a

31
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.060
limit, and objective for the goal.

32
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.960
A good model says what matters,

33
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.640
and nothing that isn’t true.

34
00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.670
Now any plan can be checked.

35
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.840
The usual plate: two cups of rice,

36
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.840
an egg and a cup of milk.

37
00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.040
Energy: seven hundred and

38
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.380
forty-five, fine.

39
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.940
Protein: twenty-four, fine.

40
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.080
Iron: only one point eight milligrams.

41
00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.910
So it isn’t feasible, at sixty coins.

42
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:00.660
Quick check!

43
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.860
A plate has one cup of beans and

44
00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.480
two handfuls of groundnuts.

45
00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.350
Energy: two hundred and thirty,

46
00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:09.930
plus three hundred and forty.

47
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.160
Does it meet the energy limit?

48
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.020
No: five hundred and seventy is

49
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.340
less than seven hundred, so the

50
00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.420
plate isn’t feasible.

51
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:21.540
Putting the numbers in is

52
00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.680
all it takes to check.

53
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.790
Finding the cheapest feasible plate

54
00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.450
is harder: the simplex method finds

55
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:30.270
it, at forty-one coins.

56
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.840
But would pupils eat it?

57
00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.490
The model knows nothing about

58
00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.470
taste, variety or allergies.

59
00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.600
Allow four handfuls, and the cheapest

60
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.140
plate is nearly all groundnuts.

61
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.450
If a rule matters, write it into the

62
00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.970
model, with the cook and the pupils.

63
00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.280
In the app, build a plate yourself

64
00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.240
and watch each need fill up.

65
00:02:57.860 --> 00:02:59.980
Serve the usual plate, then

66
00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:00.920
ask for the cheapest.

67
00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.800
Which needs are met exactly,

68
00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.300
and which come free?

69
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.240
Your turn.

70
00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.380
A colleague’s lunch model has the

71
00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.440
variables and every limit, but no goal.

72
00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.520
What will a solver give him?

73
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.180
Any plate that fits, maybe a costly one:

74
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.180
without a goal, there’s no “best”.

75
00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.520
Remember: decisions become variables,

76
00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.700
limits become inequalities, and the

77
00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.560
goal becomes one expression.

78
00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.380
Check a plan by putting its numbers in.

79
00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.580
And a model knows only

80
00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:39.800
the rules you write.

81
00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:44.110
Try it in class tomorrow: model a

82
00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:45.550
class purchase with your pupils.

83
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.600
Exercise books and pens, a budget, and

84
00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.680
how many of each the class needs.

85
00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.340
Write the variables, limits

86
00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:55.980
and goal on the board.

87
00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.560
Then check each group’s

88
00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.680
plan with the numbers.
